Specify the Identifier of a Cell [one column]
The left curly brace { designates the start of the range, followed by the number of the index of the cell,
followed by a right curly brace }.
The direct statement consists of the identifier of a cell. This identifier may be a number greater than 0, a
user-defined index or an asterisk *, which stands for all cells.
Example
{1} // first cell
{*} // all cells
Specify the Identifier of a Cell [one column]
11-3936 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Specifying a Range of Cells [one column]
The from-to statement consists of the identifiers of two cells within braces, separated by two periods.
If you do not know the identifier of the last cell, type in {*}, which designates the last cell if preceded by
two periods.
Type in to access
{1,3} the cell in column 1, row 3
{1,*} all rows in column 1
{*,3} all columns in row 3
{*,*} the entire list
Example
{2}..{4} // cells 2 to 4
{3}..{*} // from cell 3 to the last cell in the range
Specifying a Range of Cells [one column]
Plant Simulation Help 11-3937
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Address a User-defined Index [one column]
For a user-defined index of data type integer you have to add the number sign/hash # in front of the
identifier, as the system index, the numbers of rows and columns Plant Simulation assigns, also has the
data type integer.
Without the number sign, Plant Simulation would not be able to distinguish whether to use the system
index or the user-defined index.
Example
[2]  // system index
[#2] // user-defined index
Cell Ranges in Tables with Several Columns
Cell Ranges in Tables with Several Columns
Plant Simulation can process contiguous ranges of cells of tables spanning several columns. A number of
methods do not work without specifying a range.
The syntax diagram below depicts a two-dimensional range.
See also
Specify the Identifier of a Cell [several columns]
Specifying a Range of Cells [several columns]
Address a User-defined Index [several columns]
Address a User-defined Index [one column]
11-3938 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Specify the Identifier of a Cell [several columns]
To access a cell, type in the identifier of the column and the identifier of the row, separated by a comma.
The identifier for the column and row may be a number greater than 0, a user-defined index, or an asterisk
*, which stands for the entire table.
The left curly bracket { designates the start of the range, followed by the identifier of the column, a
comma, the identifier of the row, and a right curly bracket }.
Type in to access
{1,3} the cell in column 1, row 3
{1,*} all rows in column 1
{*,3} all columns in row 3
{*,*} the entire table
Example
{1,3}                  // the cell in column 1, row 3
{"vehicle","door"}     // the cell with the column index vehicle and the 
row index door
{2,*}                  // all cells in column 2
Specify the Identifier of a Cell [several columns]
Plant Simulation Help 11-3939
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Specifying a Range of Cells [several columns]
To access a range of cells, use the from-to statement. It consists of two cells located in two columns,
separated by two periods.
Remarks
The first identifier designates the top left cell identified by the number of the column and the number of
the row, the second the bottom right cell identified by the number of the column and the number of the
row. If the second identifier is {*,*}, Plant Simulation processes the table up to the highest valid column
index and row index.
Type in to access
{1,2}..{3,5} the cells starting with column 1, row 2 to column 3, row 5
{1,*}..{4,*} all rows in columns 1 to 4
{2,3}..{*,3} all columns in row 3 starting with column 2
{2,3}..{*,*} all columns starting with column 2, row 3 to the highest valid
cell
{*,*}..{3,5} all columns up to column 3 and all rows until row 5
{4,*} all rows in column 4 including the column index if Column
index belongs to contents is active
{"ColumnB", 0}..{"ColumnB",*} the column index of columnB and all rows in columnB
{1,2}..{3,5}                        // cells starting with column 1, row 2 
to column 3, row 5
{"front","door"}..{"rear","door"}   // all cells from column index front to 
rear and row index door
{2,3}..{*,*}                        // all columns starting with column 2, 
row 3 to the highest valid cell
Note
Specifying the range of cells has different effects on SimTalk instructions, depending if Column Index
Belongs to Contents is activated or not.
Column Index Belongs to Contents
